= Schema =

== Overview ==
A '''schema''' is an explicit specification of the expected or permitted structure of data, documents, messages, databases or knowledge artifacts.

A schema can define:
* fields;
* data types;
* required values;
* relationships;
* keys;
* cardinalities;
* nesting;
* constraints.

Schemas help systems validate, interpret and exchange structured information.

== Data and schema ==
A schema describes structure.

A data instance supplies particular values.

For example:

<pre>
schema:
  Person has name:string
  Person has birthDate:date

instance:
  Person123 name Alice
</pre>

The schema and the instance are different layers.

== Database schema ==
A relational database schema can define:
* tables;
* columns;
* data types;
* primary keys;
* foreign keys;
* uniqueness;
* nullability;
* indexes;
* constraints.

The schema organizes how data can be stored and related.

It does not automatically capture all of the domain meaning.

== Document schema ==
Document formats can have schemas.

Examples include:
* XML Schema;
* JSON Schema;
* Protocol Buffers schemas;
* Avro schemas.

These define permitted document/message structure.

They are often used for validation and code generation.

== Schema and validation ==
A validation schema can answer:

<blockquote>Does this data instance conform to the required structure?</blockquote>

For example, a schema may require:
* exactly one identifier;
* a date field;
* an integer in a range.

A validation failure does not necessarily mean the real-world claim is false.

It means the representation violates the schema.

== Schema and ontology ==
A [[Ontology|ontology]] primarily describes the meaning and conceptual relationships of a domain.

A schema often focuses on representation structure and constraints.

The two can overlap.

A domain model can act as both a semantic ontology and an implementation schema.

But they should not be treated as synonyms.

== RDF Schema ==
'''RDF Schema''' (RDFS) is a particularly important naming trap.

Despite the word "Schema", RDFS provides semantic vocabulary constructs such as:
* class;
* subclass;
* property;
* subproperty;
* domain;
* range.

Its domain/range statements support inference.

They are not ordinary closed-world record-validation constraints.

== SHACL and graph validation ==
RDF graphs can be validated using shape languages such as SHACL.

A shape can specify:
* required properties;
* counts;
* datatypes;
* allowed classes;
* value constraints.

This is distinct from RDFS/OWL semantic entailment.

A system can use both ontology semantics and validation shapes.

== Schema evolution ==
Schemas change.

Evolution issues include:
* adding fields;
* removing fields;
* renaming fields;
* changing types;
* splitting entities;
* changing keys.

Backward/forward compatibility depends on serialization and application rules.

Versioning should therefore be explicit.

== Schema inference ==
Some systems infer a schema from existing data.

This can reveal common structure, but observed data may be incomplete or inconsistent.

An inferred schema describes patterns in current data, not necessarily intended rules.

== Schema and metadata ==
[[Metadata]] can describe a schema itself:
* title;
* version;
* creator;
* license;
* creation date.

Metadata and schema are therefore different but complementary.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki's <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> contains compiler-facing controlled vocabularies and deterministic metadata.

It is one project schema, not the general definition of schema and not the whole BITwiki ontology.

== Epistemic notes ==
Structural validity is not semantic truth. Data can conform perfectly to a schema while encoding false or misleading claims.

